      "type": "brief",
      "date": "2026-02-07T21:49:33.000Z",
      "title": "Third quarter 2026 earnings released: ₹0.54 loss per share (vs ₹1.28 loss in 3Q 2025)",
      "body": "Third quarter 2026 results: ₹0.54 loss per share (improved from ₹1.28 loss in 3Q 2025). Revenue: ₹43.3m (up 28% from 3Q 2025). Net loss: ₹22.2m (loss narrowed 38% from 3Q 2025). Over the last 3 years on average, earnings per share has increased by 56% per year but the company’s share price has only increased by 29% per year, which means it is significantly lagging earnings growth.",
      "keyDevTypeId": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "raw_subtype": "BriefActivity"
    },
    {
      "id": "22cc884d-82bc-44f6-b144-7bba5821d8c1",
      "type": "brief",
      "date": "2026-02-04T20:55:04.000Z",
      "title": "New major risk - Shareholder dilution",
      "body": "The company's shareholders have been substantially diluted in the past year. Increase in shares outstanding: 45% This is considered a major risk. Shareholder dilution occurs when there is an increase in the number of shares on issue that is not proportionally distributed between all shareholders. Often due to the company raising equity capital or some options being converted into stock. All else being equal, if there are more shares outstanding then each existing share will be entitled to a lower proportion of the company's total earnings, thus reducing earnings per share (EPS). While dilution might not always result in lower EPS (like if the company is using the capital to fund an EPS accretive acquisition) in a lot cases it does, along with lower dividends per share and less voting power",
      "keyDevTypeId": null,
      "source_url": null,
      "raw_subtype": "BriefActivity"
    },
